Audit the Domain - Learning Windows security auditing - Setup a DC and configured advanced audit policy and Structure Notes for Lab17
 Baseline the current audit settings, then build and apply the audit GPO, used PowerShell to pull specific security events and read the fields a SOC analyst actually 
uses (failure codes, logon types).

Step-by-step breakdown

-Setup a DC	You stand up a Windows Server Domain Controller (e.g., in a VM or Hyper-V) so you have a domain to audit.
-Configured advanced audit policy	You open Group Policy → Computer Config → Policies → Windows Settings → Security Settings → Advanced Audit Policy Configuration and enable 
specific categories (Logon/Logoff, Object Access, Policy Change, etc.). This is what generates the security events in the first place. Baseline the current audit settings	
-Before changing anything, you snapshot what's already enabled (e.g., auditpol /get /category:*). This is your "before" state — if something breaks, you know what to revert 
to. 
-Build and apply the audit GPO	You create a dedicated Group Policy Object containing your audit settings, link it to the domain (or a specific OU), and force it to apply 
(gpupdate /force).
-Used PowerShell to pull specific security events	You query the Security event log with Get-WinEvent or Get-EventLog for specific event IDs — e.g., 4625 (failed logon), 4624
(successful logon), 4740 (account lockout).
-Read the fields a SOC analyst actually uses	You learn to interpret the key fields in those events: Status/SubStatus (failure codes like 0xC000006D = bad password), Logon Type
(2 = interactive, 3 = network, 10 = remote desktop), Source Network Address, Target Account Name, etc. This is what an analyst at a SOC does all day.
-Ends with a reusable query you could turn into a script	The exercise concludes with a PowerShell one-liner or small script (e.g., "pull all failed logons from the last 24 
hours, show source IP and account") that you can save, parameterise, and run repeatedly — the seed of an actual SOC detection script.

Why it's structured this way
It follows the real-world order a security engineer works in:
Infrastructure (DC exists)
Enable the signal (audit policy generates events)
Know your baseline (so you can detect changes)
Apply policy at scale (GPO, not local config)
Consume the signal (PowerShell queries)
Understand the signal (SOC-relevant fields)
Automate (reusable script)
It's essentially a mini detection engineering pipeline: generate → collect → parse → alert. The "reusable query" at the end is the bridge from "I ran a one-off 
command" to "I have a detection rule" — which is exactly what tools like Sigma, Splunk, or Elastic ingest in production.

Explained audit policy effects
In short: they tell Windows to write a record to the Security log every time a specific action happens. Before you enable them, those actions occur silently — no trace.
After, every occurrence generates a structured event you can query.

What actually changes on the system
You enable…	What happens from that moment on
Audit Logon (Success + Failure)	Every sign-in attempt (interactive, RDP, service, network) writes a 4624 or 4625 event to the Security log on the machine being accessed.
Audit Account Logon (Kerberos, Credential Validation)	Every TGT request, service ticket request, or NTLM validation on the DC writes events like 4768, 4769, 4771, 4776.
Audit Account Management	Any time someone creates, modifies, disables, or deletes a user/computer account or group membership, an event fires (4720, 4722, 4738, 4732,etc.).
Audit Policy Change	Any modification to audit policy, GPO, or local security settings is logged (4719, 4742, 4739).
Audit Object Access + SACL on a file/folder	Every read/write/delete on that specific object is logged (4663, 4656, 4660).
Audit Process Creation	Every new process launch writes a 4688 event (with command line if you also enable that sub-setting). 
Audit Privilege Use	Any time a process calls a sensitive privilege (SeBackupPrivilege, SeDebugPrivilege, etc.), a 4673 event is written.

The practical effect in one sentence
You go from "blind" (actions happen, no record) to "visible" (every action you chose to track leaves a timestamped, structured, queryable event in the Security log).

Important nuances
Enabling the policy alone isn't always enough. For Object Access, you also need a SACL (System Access Control List) on the specific file/folder/registry key. The policy 
says "if an audited object is touched, log it" — the SACL says "this object is audited."
Success vs. Failure — you choose which to log.  A common mistake is enabling only Failure and thinking you're covered. Many of the most important events (account changes,
group membership changes, policy changes) are Success events. Volume matters.
Enabling everything indiscriminately (especially Object Access or Process Creation with command lines) can flood the Security log and bury the signal. That's why the exercise uses Advanced (subcategory-level) rather than the legacy Basic (category-level) policy — you pick exactly which subcategories to turn on. 
The GPO is the delivery mechanism. You're not editing each machine locally; the GPO pushes the audit config to every computer in the linked OU. gpupdate /force makes it 
apply immediately rather than waiting for the next refresh cycle. The log rolls. The local Security log has a fixed size (default ~20 MB). If you generate more events than
it can hold, old events are overwritten (or the log stops, depending on the overflow policy). This is why real deployments forward events to a SIEM (Splunk, Elastic, Sentinel
,etc.) in near-real-time. So the "change" is: Windows now has a mandate to record specific actions, and the GPO ensures that mandate is applied consistently across the 
domain rather than being a one-off local tweak.


